Sec. 70501 terminates the previously-owned clean vehicle credit by moving the deadline from December 31, 2032 to September 30, 2025, meaning vehicles acquired after that date will no longer qualify for the credit under Section 25E.

Sec. 70502 terminates the clean vehicle credit under Section 30D by changing the placed-in-service deadline from December 31, 2032 to an acquisition deadline of September 30, 2025, and makes conforming amendments to eliminate certain vehicle categories from eligibility.

Sec. 70503 terminates the qualified commercial clean vehicles credit by moving the deadline from December 31, 2032 to September 30, 2025, so commercial clean vehicles acquired after that date will no longer qualify under Section 45W.

Sec. 70504 terminates the alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit by moving the deadline from December 31, 2032 to June 30, 2026, meaning refueling property placed in service after that date will not qualify under Section 30C.

Sec. 70505 terminates the energy efficient home improvement credit for property placed in service after December 31, 2025, and modifies the definition of qualifying oil furnaces and hot water boilers to require they meet 2021 Energy Star efficiency criteria and be rated for use with fuel blends containing at least 20 percent eligible fuel.

Sec. 70506 terminates the residential clean energy credit for expenditures made after December 31, 2025, and makes conforming amendments to eliminate the reduced credit percentages that would have applied in later years under Section 25D.

Sec. 70507 terminates the energy efficient commercial buildings deduction by adding a new subsection (i) to Section 179D providing that the section shall not apply to property whose construction begins after June 30, 2026.

Sec. 70508 terminates the new energy efficient home credit by moving the deadline from December 31, 2032 to June 30, 2026, so homes acquired after that date will no longer qualify under Section 45L.

Sec. 70509 terminates cost recovery for energy property by amending Section 168(e)(3)(B)(vi) to remove certain energy property from 15-year recovery treatment, effective for property whose construction begins after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70510 modifies the zero-emission nuclear power production credit by denying the credit to specified foreign entities (as defined in Section 7701(a)(51)(B)) for taxable years beginning after enactment, and to foreign-influenced entities for taxable years beginning two years after enactment.

Sec. 70511 terminates the clean hydrogen production credit by moving the deadline from January 1, 2033 to January 1, 2028, so facilities placed in service after that date will no longer qualify under Section 45V.

Sec. 70512 terminates and restricts the clean electricity production credit: wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027 lose eligibility; facilities whose construction begins after December 31, 2025 cannot receive material assistance from prohibited foreign entities; specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities are denied the credit; and new definitions of "prohibited foreign entity" and "material assistance" are added to Section 7701(a), including ownership thresholds of 25 percent (single entity) and 40 percent (aggregate), debt thresholds of 15 percent, and material assistance cost ratio thresholds ranging from 40 to 75 percent depending on year and technology.

Sec. 70513 terminates and restricts the clean electricity investment credit: wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027 lose eligibility (except energy storage technology); facilities whose construction begins after December 31, 2025 cannot receive material assistance from prohibited foreign entities; specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities are denied the credit; recapture rules apply to applicable payments to prohibited foreign entities; domestic content percentages are set at 40 percent (20 percent offshore wind) for construction beginning before June 16, 2025, rising to 55 percent after December 31, 2026; and the energy credit for certain property is reduced to 0 percent.

Sec. 70514 phases out and restricts the advanced manufacturing production credit: the phase-out for applicable critical minerals (other than metallurgical coal) begins after December 31, 2030 at 75 percent, declining to 50 percent in 2032, 25 percent in 2033, and 0 percent after 2033; wind energy components terminate after December 31, 2027; metallurgical coal terminates after December 31, 2029; material assistance from prohibited foreign entities disqualifies eligible components; and metallurgical coal is added as an applicable critical mineral with a 2.5 percent credit rate.

Sec. 70515 restricts the extension of the advanced energy project credit program by amending Section 48C(e)(3)(C) to provide that the program's allocation shall not be increased, effective on the date of enactment.

Sec. 70521 extends and modifies the clean fuel production credit: fuel must be exclusively derived from feedstocks produced or grown in the United States, Mexico, or Canada; negative emission rates are prohibited (except for animal manure-derived fuel); indirect land use change emissions are excluded; the credit is extended from December 31, 2027 to December 31, 2029; double crediting is prevented; the sustainable aviation fuel special rate is eliminated; the small agri-biodiesel producer credit is doubled from 10 to 20 cents and extended; and specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities are denied the credit.

Sec. 70522 restricts the carbon oxide sequestration credit by denying it to specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities for taxable years beginning after enactment, and equalizes credit rates across different uses and utilizations of qualified carbon oxide (geological storage, tertiary injectants, and other utilization) by setting the applicable dollar amount at $17 for 2025-2026 (inflation-adjusted thereafter) and $36 for certain facilities.

Sec. 70523 modifies the computation of adjusted financial statement income under Section 56A(c)(13) to allow deductions for depreciation under Section 167 and for intangible drilling and development costs under Section 263(c), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70524 expands qualifying income for publicly traded partnerships under Section 7704(d)(1)(E) to include income from hydrogen storage, carbon capture (facilities capturing at least 50 percent qualified carbon oxide), advanced nuclear facilities, hydropower and geothermal energy production, and certain energy property, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70525 allows payments to certain individuals who dye fuel by adding new Section 6435, which provides that a person who removes eligible indelibly dyed diesel fuel or kerosene from a terminal (on which tax under Section 4081 was previously paid and which is exempt under Section 4082(a)) shall receive a payment equal to the tax, effective for fuel removed 180 days after enactment.

Sec. 70531 modifies the de minimis entry privilege for commercial shipments by imposing a civil penalty of up to $5,000 for the first violation and up to $10,000 for each subsequent violation for persons who use the privilege to import articles violating customs law (effective 30 days after enactment), and repeals the commercial shipment exception to the $800 de minimis threshold effective July 1, 2027.

Sec. 70601 makes permanent the limitation on excess business losses of noncorporate taxpayers (removing the 2029 sunset) and adjusts the amounts for calculating excess business losses by updating the 2018 reference date to December 31, 2025 and the 2017 inflation adjustment base year to 2024, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025 (adjustments) and December 31, 2026 (permanence).

Sec. 70602 modifies the treatment of payments from partnerships to partners for property or services under Section 707(a)(2) by striking "Under regulations prescribed" and inserting "Except as provided," effective for services performed and property transferred after enactment, with a rule of construction that the change creates no inference regarding prior treatment.

Sec. 70603 addresses excessive employee remuneration from controlled group members by adding Section 162(m)(7), which applies the $1,000,000 deduction limitation to specified covered employees across controlled group members and allocates the limitation amount among members based on each member's share of applicable employee remuneration, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70604 imposes a new excise tax on certain remittance transfers equal to 1 percent of the transfer amount, paid by the sender and collected by the remittance transfer provider for quarterly remittance to the Secretary, applying only to cash, money orders, cashier's checks, or similar physical instruments (not bank account withdrawals or debit/credit card transfers), effective for transfers made after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70605 establishes enforcement provisions for COVID-related employee retention credits: COVID-ERTC promoters failing due diligence requirements pay a $1,000 penalty per failure; promoters are defined by fee-based compensation and gross receipt thresholds (20 percent or 50 percent, or 20 percent plus $500,000); no credits or refunds allowed after enactment unless claimed by January 31, 2024; the assessment limitation period is extended to 6 years; and the erroneous claim penalty is expanded to employment tax.

Sec. 70606 requires a social security number for the American Opportunity and Lifetime Learning credits under Section 25A(g)(1), requires the institution's employer identification number for the American Opportunity Tax Credit, treats omission as a mathematical or clerical error, and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70607 appropriates $15,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for the Department of the Treasury to deliver a report to Congress within 90 days of enactment on the cost of public-private partnerships providing free tax filing for up to 70 percent of taxpayers by adjusted gross income, taxpayer preferences, feasibility of a new approach, and the cost of developing a free direct e-file tax return system to replace IRS Direct File. [end of text]


